/* data/learn-c/phy-11-04.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-11-04.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-04"] = {"4.1":{"s":"This chapter links force to motion. It gives Newton's three laws, the idea of a frame of reference, the main kinds of force, and then work and energy, momentum, collisions, torque, equilibrium and the centre of mass. These ideas explain how objects move, push each other and stay balanced.","w":["Force changes the state of motion; it is not needed to keep an object moving at constant velocity.","Work is done only when there is displacement along the force, not just when a force acts."],"x":["Kinematics tells how things move. Dynamics tells why. This chapter links force to motion. It gives Newton's three laws of motion, the idea of a frame of reference, the main kinds of force, and then work and energy, momentum, collisions, torque, equilibrium and the centre of mass.","These ideas explain how objects speed up and slow down, how they push on each other, why a rocket moves, how a bat hits a ball, why a door turns about its hinge, and how a body can stay balanced. It is one of the most important chapters of Class 11, and many later topics, like gravitation and oscillations, depend on it.","Study tip: for every problem, draw a free body diagram. Show only the forces that act on that body, choose a positive direction, and then write Newton's second law along each direction."],"e":"A book rests on a table. Two forces act on the book: its weight, down, and the normal reaction of the table, up. They are equal, so the net force is zero, and the book stays at rest."},"4.2":{"s":"Aristotle believed a force is needed to keep a body moving, because moving things stop when you stop pushing. This is wrong. Galileo showed that things stop because friction, air drag or other opposing forces act on them. A body would go on moving at constant velocity if no unbalanced force acted on it, which is the idea behind Newton's first law.","w":["Constant velocity needs no net force; a force is needed only to CHANGE the velocity."],"x":["Aristotle believed that a force is needed to keep a body moving, because everyday things stop when we stop pushing them. For about two thousand years, this idea was accepted. But it is wrong.","Galileo studied balls rolling on smooth slopes. The smoother the surface, the farther the ball rolled. He reasoned that on a perfectly smooth, level surface, the ball would never stop. Things in daily life stop only because friction, air drag or some other opposing force acts on them. If no unbalanced force acts, a body keeps its velocity. This idea is the law of inertia, which became Newton's first law.","The right way to think: a force is needed to change the velocity (to start, stop, speed up, slow down or turn a body), but it is not needed to keep a constant velocity. A car at a steady speed on a level road has a forward force from the engine that exactly balances friction and drag, so the net force is zero.","Common mistake: thinking that a body that moves must have a net force on it in the direction of the motion."],"e":"A hockey puck slides on ice and slowly stops. The reason is the small friction. On an ideal frictionless surface, it would slide for ever at a steady speed, with no force needed."},"4.3":{"s":"First law: a body stays at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless an unbalanced force acts on it. Second law: the rate of change of momentum is proportional to the applied force, and it follows the direction of the force. For constant mass this gives force equals mass times acceleration. Third law: to every action there is an equal and opposite reaction, acting on different bodies.","f":[{"x":"F = dp/dt","n":"Second law: force = rate of change of linear momentum p = m*v.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.3, p.48"},{"x":"F = m*a","n":"Second law for a body of constant mass m (not valid when the mass changes, as in a rocket).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.3, p.48"}],"w":["Action and reaction act on DIFFERENT bodies, so they never cancel each other.","F = ma is a special case: when mass changes, use F = dp/dt."],"x":["First law: a body stays at rest, or in uniform motion in a straight line, unless an unbalanced force acts on it. This property is called inertia, and mass is its measure. Second law: the net force on a body decides how fast its momentum changes, and the change is along the force: F = dp/dt. For a constant mass, this gives F = ma. Third law: for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.","Using the second law: write the net force along a chosen direction, with signs, and equate it to mass times acceleration. If the mass changes, as in a rocket, use F = dp/dt. The unit of force is the newton, 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻². The momentum is p = mv.","The action and the reaction of the third law act on different bodies. So they never cancel each other. A horse pulls a cart, and the cart pulls the horse back with an equal force. The horse moves because the ground pushes the horse forward with a force greater than the pull from the cart.","Common mistake: adding the action and the reaction forces on the same free body diagram."],"e":"A force of 20 N acts on a 5 kg body at rest. a = F/m = 4 m/s². After 3 s, v = 12 m/s. The change of momentum is 5 × 12 = 60 kg m/s, which equals the impulse Ft = 20 × 3 = 60."},"4.4":{"s":"Motion is relative, so we describe it from a frame of reference. A frame that is at rest or moves at constant velocity is an inertial frame, and Newton's laws hold in it. A frame that accelerates, such as a turning bus, is non-inertial. In such a frame bodies seem to feel extra forces that do not come from any real interaction.","w":["Newton's laws hold only in inertial frames. In an accelerating frame you must add a pseudo force.","A pseudo force is not caused by any object; it is felt only because the frame accelerates."],"x":["Motion is relative. To describe a motion, we need a frame of reference, such as a set of axes fixed to a laboratory, a car or the ground. A frame that is at rest or moves with a constant velocity is an inertial frame, and Newton's laws are true in it. A frame that is accelerating, like a bus that is braking or turning, or a rotating frame, is a non-inertial frame.","An observer in a non-inertial frame finds that Newton's laws do not work in their simple form. For example, in a bus that brakes suddenly, a passenger is thrown forward, although no real object pushes. The explanation for an observer on the ground is simple: the passenger just goes on in a straight line (inertia), and the bus slows down under the passenger.","The Earth is not an exactly inertial frame, because it rotates and goes round the Sun, but for most problems, a frame fixed to the Earth is a good approximation.","Common mistake: using Newton's laws in a non-inertial frame without adding a pseudo force. Either work in an inertial frame, or include the pseudo force."],"e":"A ball is placed on the smooth floor of a bus. When the bus accelerates forward, the ball rolls to the back, as seen from the bus. A person on the road sees the ball stay at rest, while the bus moves forward under it."},"4.5":{"s":"Forces can be gravitational, electromagnetic, strong nuclear or weak nuclear. Contact forces (normal reaction, friction, tension) are electromagnetic in origin. In a non-inertial frame we add a pseudo force, equal to minus mass times the frame's acceleration, so that Newton's second law still works. A lift accelerating upward makes your apparent weight larger. Work done against a conservative force is stored as potential energy.","f":[{"x":"g = G*M/R^2","n":"Acceleration due to gravity at the Earth's surface; M and R = mass and radius of the Earth.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.51"},{"x":"F_pseudo = -m*a","n":"Pseudo force on a body of mass m in a frame accelerating with acceleration a.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.53"},{"x":"W_apparent = m*(g + a)","n":"Apparent weight in a lift accelerating UPWARD with a; use m*(g - a) when it accelerates downward.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.53"},{"x":"W = F*s*cos(theta)","n":"Work done by a constant force F over displacement s; theta = angle between F and s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.5, p.54"}],"w":["Pseudo forces appear only in accelerating frames; they have no reaction partner.","In a lift, apparent weight is m(g + a) going up faster or slowing while going down, and m(g − a) in the opposite cases."],"x":["In nature, there are four basic forces: the gravitational force, the electromagnetic force, the strong nuclear force and the weak nuclear force. From the weakest to the strongest, the order is gravitational, weak nuclear, electromagnetic and strong nuclear. All the contact forces that we use, like the normal reaction, friction and tension, come from the electromagnetic force between atoms.","The gravitational field of a planet of mass M and radius R gives the acceleration due to gravity at its surface, g = GM/R². The work done by a force F over a displacement s at an angle θ to the force is W = Fs cos θ.","In a frame with an acceleration a, we add a pseudo force, F = −ma, to every body of mass m, so that Newton's second law works again. It has no reaction partner. A lift with an upward acceleration a: the apparent weight, which is the reading of a scale, is m(g + a). With a downward acceleration a, it is m(g − a). In free fall, a = g and the reading is zero, which is weightlessness. At a constant velocity, the reading is mg.","Common mistake: using mg as the scale reading in an accelerating lift."],"e":"A person of 60 kg stands on a scale in a lift. When the lift goes up with a = 2 m/s², the reading is 60 × (10 + 2) = 720 N. When it goes down with a = 2 m/s², the reading is 60 × (10 − 2) = 480 N."},"4.6":{"s":"The net work done on a body by all forces equals the change in its kinetic energy. A force along the motion increases the speed and does positive work; an opposing force decreases it. With friction or air drag, which are non-conservative, some work is lost as heat, so the mechanical energy is not conserved.","f":[{"x":"(1/2)*m*v^2 - (1/2)*m*u^2 = F*s","n":"Work-energy theorem for a constant force F along a straight line: u = initial and v = final speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.6, p.55"}],"w":["Include ALL forces in the work, including friction; a force at right angles to the motion does no work."],"x":["The work-energy theorem says that the net work done on a body by all the forces acting on it is equal to the change in its kinetic energy: W_net = (1/2)mv² − (1/2)mu². A force along the motion does positive work and increases the speed. An opposing force does negative work and decreases the speed. A force at right angles to the motion does no work, like the centripetal force in uniform circular motion.","How to use it: make a list of all the forces, find the work of each one with W = Fs cos θ, add them up with signs, and equate the sum to the change of kinetic energy. It is often quicker than using the equations of motion, especially when the force is not constant or the path is curved, because it needs only the start and the end.","Friction and air drag are non-conservative forces. They take some of the mechanical energy and change it to heat, so the mechanical energy is not conserved when they act. The work-energy theorem still holds, if the work of friction is included.","Common mistake: leaving out the work done by friction, or taking the work of a force that is at right angles to the displacement as non-zero."],"e":"A car of mass 1000 kg speeds up from 10 m/s to 20 m/s. W_net = (1/2)(1000)(400 − 100) = 150 000 J. If this happens over 150 m, the net force is 150 000/150 = 1000 N."},"4.7":{"s":"If no net external force acts on a system, its total linear momentum stays constant. The forces between the parts (internal forces) do not change the total. This holds for collisions and explosions. To solve problems with several bodies, draw a free body diagram for each: show only the forces acting on that body, then write Newton's second law for it.","f":[{"x":"a = (m_2 - m_1)*g/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Atwood machine (light string over a pulley): acceleration of masses m_1 and m_2, with m_2 > m_1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.7, p.57"},{"x":"T = 2*m_1*m_2*g/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Tension in the string of the Atwood machine.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.7, p.58"}],"w":["Momentum is conserved only for the whole system: add the momenta of all bodies, with signs.","A free body diagram shows forces ON the body, never the forces it exerts on others."],"x":["The law of conservation of linear momentum: if the net external force on a system is zero, the total linear momentum of the system stays constant. The forces between the parts of the system, the internal forces, are equal and opposite by the third law, so they cannot change the total. The law is used for collisions, explosions and recoil.","How to use it: choose a positive direction. Add the momenta of all the bodies before, with signs, and equate to the sum after. For a gun and a bullet, the total momentum before is zero, so the recoil speed of the gun is v = (m_bullet × v_bullet)/m_gun, in the opposite direction.","For bodies joined by strings and pulleys, draw a free body diagram for each body. Show only the forces that act on it. Write the second law for each, and solve together. For two masses on the two sides of a smooth pulley, the acceleration is a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂), and the tension is T = 2m₁m₂g/(m₁ + m₂).","Common mistakes: forgetting the signs of the velocities, and drawing the forces that the body exerts on others in its own free body diagram."],"e":"A gun of 5 kg fires a bullet of 0.05 kg at 300 m/s. The momentum of the bullet is 15 kg m/s, so the gun recoils at 15/5 = 3 m/s. The kinetic energy of the bullet is 2250 J, but that of the gun is only 22.5 J."},"4.8":{"s":"In a collision, total momentum is always conserved. In an elastic collision kinetic energy is also conserved. In an inelastic collision some kinetic energy is lost. In a perfectly inelastic collision the bodies stick together and the loss is greatest. The coefficient of restitution e, the ratio of the speed of separation to the speed of approach, is 1 for elastic and 0 for perfectly inelastic, and between 0 and 1 otherwise.","f":[{"x":"e = (v_2 - v_1)/(u_1 - u_2)","n":"Coefficient of restitution for a head-on collision: u = speeds before, v = speeds after.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.59"},{"x":"v_1 = ((m_1 - m_2)/(m_1 + m_2))*u_1 + (2*m_2/(m_1 + m_2))*u_2","n":"Final velocity of m_1 after a head-on ELASTIC collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.60"},{"x":"v_2 = ((m_2 - m_1)/(m_1 + m_2))*u_2 + (2*m_1/(m_1 + m_2))*u_1","n":"Final velocity of m_2 after the same elastic collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.60"},{"x":"v = (m_1*u_1 + m_2*u_2)/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Common velocity after a perfectly inelastic collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.61"},{"x":"Delta KE = (1/2)*(m_1*m_2/(m_1 + m_2))*(u_1 - u_2)^2","n":"Kinetic energy lost in a perfectly inelastic head-on collision.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.8, p.61"}],"w":["Put signs on the velocities: take one direction as positive before using the formulas.","Equal masses in a head-on elastic collision simply swap velocities."],"x":["In a collision, the total linear momentum is always conserved, if no external force acts. In an elastic collision, the kinetic energy is also conserved. In an inelastic collision, part of the kinetic energy changes into heat and sound. In a perfectly inelastic collision, the bodies stick together, and the loss of kinetic energy is the greatest.","The coefficient of restitution is e = (speed of separation)/(speed of approach) = (v₂ − v₁)/(u₁ − u₂). It is 1 for an elastic collision, 0 for a perfectly inelastic one, and between 0 and 1 for other collisions.","Formulas for a head-on collision of m₁ and m₂, with velocities u₁ and u₂ before: for an elastic collision, v₁ = ((m₁ − m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂), and v₂ = ((m₂ − m₁)u₂ + 2m₁u₁)/(m₁ + m₂). For a perfectly inelastic collision, v = (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂), and the loss is (1/2)(m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂))(u₁ − u₂)². If the masses are equal and the collision is elastic, the bodies exchange velocities.","Common mistake: using the conservation of kinetic energy for an inelastic collision. Give a sign to each velocity before you use the formulas."],"e":"A 2 kg body at 6 m/s hits a 4 kg body at rest. If they stick, v = 12/6 = 2 m/s and the loss is 36 − 12 = 24 J. If the collision is elastic, the 2 kg body goes back at 2 m/s and the 4 kg body moves at 4 m/s."},"4.9":{"s":"Impulse is the effect of a large force acting for a very short time, such as a bat hitting a ball. It equals the force multiplied by the time, and it is equal to the change in momentum. For a force that changes with time, the impulse is the area under the force-time graph. Impulse is a vector in the direction of the force.","f":[{"x":"J = F*t = p_2 - p_1","n":"Impulse of a constant force F acting for time t: equal to the change of momentum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.9, p.63"},{"x":"J = integral of F dt","n":"Impulse of a force that changes with time: the area under the F-t graph.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.9, p.63"}],"w":["A longer contact time means a smaller average force for the same change in momentum: this is why a cricketer draws the hands back."],"x":["Impulse is the effect of a large force that acts for a very short time, like a bat hitting a ball. It is defined as J = F t for a constant force, and for a force that varies with time, J = ∫F dt, which is the area under the force-time graph. By the second law, the impulse is equal to the change of momentum: J = p₂ − p₁. It is a vector, along the direction of the force, and its unit is N s, the same as kg m s⁻¹.","Because the contact time is very short and hard to measure, it is often easier to find the impulse from the change of momentum, and then find the average force from F_avg = J/Δt.","For the same change of momentum, a longer time of contact gives a smaller average force. This is why a cricketer pulls the hands back while catching a fast ball, why a car has crumple zones, why a person bends the knees on landing, and why a mattress is used for a high jump.","Common mistake: forgetting to treat the reversal of direction. If a ball arrives at 12 m/s and leaves at 20 m/s in the opposite direction, the change of velocity is 32 m/s, not 8 m/s."],"e":"A ball of 0.15 kg arrives at 12 m/s and goes back at 20 m/s. Δp = 0.15 × 32 = 4.8 N s. If the contact lasts 0.01 s, the average force is 4.8/0.01 = 480 N."},"4.10":{"s":"Torque is the turning effect of a force about an axis. Its size is the force times the perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of the force, so it depends on where and in which direction the force acts. It is largest when the force is at 90° to the line from the axis. It is zero when the force passes through the axis, and it is a vector found with the right hand rule.","f":[{"x":"tau = r*F*sin(theta)","n":"Torque of a force F at position r from the axis; theta = smaller angle between r and F. Vector form: tau = r × F.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.10, p.66"}],"w":["A force through the axis (θ = 0° or 180°) gives zero torque, however large.","Torque depends on the chosen axis; always state which one."],"x":["Torque is the turning effect of a force about an axis. It is the rotational analogue of force. The magnitude is τ = rF sin θ, where r is the distance from the axis to the point where the force acts, and θ is the angle between r and F. Equivalently, it is the force times the perpendicular distance from the axis to the line of action of the force (the lever arm): τ = F d. In vector form, τ = r × F. The unit is N m.","The torque is the largest when the force is at 90° to the line from the axis, and it is zero when the force passes through the axis (θ = 0° or 180°). This is why you cannot open a door by pushing at the hinge, and why a long spanner makes it easier to turn a nut, since it gives a bigger r.","Torque depends on the chosen axis, so always say which axis you use. An anticlockwise torque is taken as positive, and a clockwise one as negative.","Common mistake: using the whole force instead of the component at right angles to r, or measuring the distance to the wrong point."],"e":"A force of 20 N acts at the end of a 0.50 m spanner, at 30° to it. τ = 0.50 × 20 × sin 30° = 5.0 N m. If the force is at 90° to the spanner, τ = 10 N m."},"4.11":{"s":"A couple is a pair of equal and opposite forces whose lines of action are different. It produces pure rotation and no translation, because the forces add to zero. The moment of a couple is the force times the perpendicular distance between the two lines, and it is the same about every axis. A couple can be balanced only by another couple.","f":[{"x":"tau = F*r","n":"Moment of a couple: F = size of each force, r = perpendicular distance between their lines of action.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.11, p.66"}],"w":["The torque of a couple does not depend on the axis chosen or on where the forces are applied."],"x":["A couple is a pair of forces that are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, but whose lines of action are different. Because the two forces add up to zero, a couple gives no translation. It produces a pure rotation. Turning a steering wheel with two hands, a key in a lock, or a tap are common examples.","The moment (torque) of a couple is the magnitude of one force times the perpendicular distance between the two lines of action: τ = F d. An important fact is that this torque is the same about every axis, and it does not depend on where the forces are applied. A couple can be balanced only by another couple, with an equal and opposite moment, and not by a single force.","To check this, take the two forces F at A and −F at B. About any point O, the total torque is r_A × F + r_B × (−F) = (r_A − r_B) × F. It depends only on the separation vector between the forces.","Common mistake: using the distance from an axis, rather than the distance between the two lines of action."],"e":"Two forces of 15 N each act at opposite ends of a bar that is 0.40 m long, at right angles to it. The moment of the couple is 15 × 0.40 = 6.0 N m. The net force is zero."},"4.12":{"s":"A body is in mechanical equilibrium when the net force on it is zero, so it has no acceleration. For a rigid body the net torque must also be zero. Equilibrium can be stable, unstable or neutral. In stable equilibrium the potential energy is a minimum, and a small push makes the body return. In unstable equilibrium it is a maximum, and in neutral equilibrium it stays constant.","f":[{"x":"Sum of F = 0","n":"Condition for translational equilibrium; for a rigid body also the sum of torques = 0.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.12, p.67"},{"x":"dU/dx = 0, d^2U/dx^2 > 0 (stable), d^2U/dx^2 < 0 (unstable)","n":"Equilibrium positions from the potential energy U(x); second derivative zero means neutral.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.12, p.68"}],"w":["For a ladder or a beam, use both conditions: forces balance AND torques about any point balance."],"x":["A body is in mechanical equilibrium when the net force on it is zero, so it has no linear acceleration. For an extended rigid body, the net torque about any axis must also be zero, so that it has no angular acceleration. Both conditions must be met. A body in equilibrium may be at rest (static equilibrium) or moving with a constant velocity.","How to solve a problem on a beam or a ladder: draw all the forces, such as weights acting at the centre, the reactions at the supports, and the friction. Write that the sum of the forces in each direction is zero. Then take torques about a convenient point, usually a point where an unknown force acts, to remove it from the equation. Solve for the unknowns.","Equilibrium may be stable, unstable or neutral. In stable equilibrium, the potential energy is a minimum, and a small push makes the body return (a ball at the bottom of a bowl). In unstable equilibrium, it is a maximum, and a small push makes the body move farther away (a ball on the top of a hill). In neutral equilibrium, the potential energy is constant (a ball on a flat table). In terms of the potential energy U, dU/dx = 0 and d²U/dx² is positive for stable, and negative for unstable equilibrium.","Common mistake: balancing only the forces and forgetting the torques."],"e":"A uniform plank of 6 m and 30 kg rests on two supports at its ends. A 60 kg person stands 2 m from the left end. Torques about the right end: R_L × 6 = 300 × 3 + 600 × 4, so R_L = 550 N. Then R_R = 900 − 550 = 350 N."},"4.13":{"s":"The centre of mass is the point where the whole mass of a system can be treated as concentrated when applying Newton's laws. For particles it is the mass-weighted average of positions; for a continuous body, use integration. It lies closer to the heavier mass. For a symmetric body of uniform density it is at the geometric centre. It need not lie inside the body, and only external forces change its motion.","f":[{"x":"x_cm = (m_1*x_1 + m_2*x_2 + ... + m_n*x_n)/M","n":"Centre of mass of n particles along x; M = total mass. Same form for y and z.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.69"},{"x":"x_cm = (m_1*x_1 + m_2*x_2)/(m_1 + m_2)","n":"Two particles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.71"},{"x":"v_cm = (m_1*v_1 + m_2*v_2 + ...)/M","n":"Velocity of the centre of mass: total momentum divided by total mass.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.72"},{"x":"y = 4*R/(3*pi)","n":"Centre of mass of a thin semicircular disc of radius R, measured from the diameter. A thin semicircular ring has 2R/pi.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.70"},{"x":"y = 3*R/8","n":"Solid hemisphere of radius R, from the flat face. A hemispherical shell has R/2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.70"},{"x":"y = h/4 (solid cone), y = h/3 (hollow cone)","n":"Right circular cone of height h, measured from the base.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 4.13, p.70"}],"w":["Choose the origin and the positive directions first, and give each coordinate its sign.","For a body with a hole, treat the hole as a negative mass."],"x":["The centre of mass of a system is the point at which the whole mass can be taken as concentrated when applying Newton's laws to the motion of the system as a whole. For particles, it is the mass-weighted average of the positions: x_cm = (m₁x₁ + m₂x₂ + … + mₙxₙ)/M, and the same for y and z. Its velocity is v_cm = (m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ + …)/M. If no external force acts, the centre of mass moves with a constant velocity, even when the parts move in complicated ways.","How to find it: choose an origin and the positive directions first, give each coordinate its sign, and use the formula. For two particles, the centre of mass is on the line joining them, closer to the heavier one: x_cm = (m₁x₁ + m₂x₂)/(m₁ + m₂). For a body with a hole, treat the hole as a body with a negative mass.","For a uniform body, use symmetry first: the centre of mass of a rod, a ring, a disc, a sphere or a rectangle is at its geometric centre. Standard results: a solid semicircular disc, 4R/(3π) from the centre of the straight edge; a semicircular ring, 2R/π; a solid hemisphere, 3R/8 from the centre of the flat face; a solid cone, h/4 from the base; and a hollow cone, h/3 from the base.","Common mistake: taking the sign of a coordinate wrongly, and mixing up the results for the solid and the hollow bodies."],"e":"Masses 1 kg, 2 kg and 3 kg are at x = 0, 1 m and 3 m. x_cm = (0 + 2 + 9)/6 = 1.83 m. A solid semicircular disc of R = 0.30 m has its centre of mass at 4 × 0.30/(3π) = 0.127 m from the straight edge."},"4.14":{"s":"The centre of gravity is the point where the total weight of a body acts, the point about which the torque of gravity is zero. For a uniform gravitational field it coincides with the centre of mass. The two differ only when gravity varies over the body, which is negligible for ordinary objects. A suspended body hangs with its centre of gravity vertically below the point of support.","f":[{"x":"x_cm = Σmᵢxᵢ / Σmᵢ","n":"Centre of mass of particles. The same form holds for y and z."}],"w":["Centre of gravity and centre of mass are the same point in a uniform gravitational field."],"x":["The centre of gravity of a body is the point at which the whole weight of the body acts. It is also the point about which the total torque of the gravitational forces on all the parts of the body is zero. If you support a body at this point, it stays balanced in any position.","In a uniform gravitational field, the weight of each part is proportional to its mass, so the centre of gravity and the centre of mass are the same point. They differ only if the gravitational field changes over the size of the body, which is true only for very large bodies, and the difference is negligible for ordinary objects.","A body that is hung freely from a point comes to rest with its centre of gravity vertically below the point of suspension. This gives a way to find the centre of gravity of a flat sheet: hang it from two different points, mark the vertical line under each point, and the lines cross at the centre of gravity.","A body is stable when its centre of gravity is low and the vertical line from it falls inside the base. That is why a wide, low object is hard to tip over. Common mistake: thinking that the centre of gravity must be inside the material of the body. A ring has it at its centre, in the hole."],"e":"A flat sheet of an irregular shape is hung from a pin at one corner, and a plumb line is marked. It is hung from another corner, and a second line is marked. The crossing point of the two lines is the centre of gravity."}};
